sequenceDiagram
  %% Container runtime (ADR 0250 step 8, docs/reference/development-containers.md#sleep-and-wake): freeze a development container with its memory in swap and leave its Route on the wake page. Defaults and nested-fleet Instances sleep under the same rule as clones (owner decision 2026-11-14). The hibernation sweep admits the same kind after an idle hour; the command admits it at once.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: instance:sleep
  %% kind: instance.sleep
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · sweep
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>Instance container
  CLI->>GW: instance:sleep INSTANCE
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit instance:sleep INSTANCE`
  %%> - syntax: `<instance-id> [--json]`
  %%> Callers:
  %%> - the command, at once
  %%> - the idle sleep of the runtime hibernator, every 10 min, after it read no HTTP or wake activity for an hour and, for an Instance with a T3 server, no T3 activity and no running turn (docs/reference/development-containers.md#sleep-and-wake)
  opt the Instance has a T3 server
    Note over GW: Last read of T3 threads<br/>Conn thread index
    %%> When:
    %%> - after the refusals and the replay of a repeated request, before the Instance lock; within 3 seconds
    %%> - only while Conn and its index switch `ORBIT_CONN_THREAD_INDEX_POLL` are on; the sleep setting does not matter
    %%> - a default has no T3 server: nothing to read
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - reads the container T3 server's threads once more, so the index keeps them while the Instance sleeps (docs/reference/conn.md#thread-index)
    %%> On failure:
    %%> - the index keeps its earlier rows; the sleep goes on
  end
  Note over GW: Admit the sleep
  %%> When:
  %%> - an active development container, a default and a nested-fleet Instance included; take the Instance lock
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - no container, a non-development Instance, an active task or lifecycle work starts no run
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-sleep
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID
  SW->>N: Mark the Route asleep<br/>Ansible · tags asleep
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - checks that Caddy's loaded site of the Instance has the asleep rule, else `instance.sleep_route_unready`
  %%> - writes the asleep marker, so each request from now on gets the wake page
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the marker comes before the freeze: a request during the freeze gets the wake page, not a hang
  SW->>N: Freeze the container<br/>Ansible · tags freeze
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - writes `max` to `memory.swap.max` of every cgroup in the container's subtree that holds 0: the container's own (Incus writes 0) and, in a nested-fleet Instance, the inner payload cgroups (the inner Incus writes 0 too)
  %%> - records the inner cgroups it opened on the Node, `/dev/shm/orbit/hibernation/app-instance-<id>.swap`; a cgroup with its own limit, such as 32 MiB, stays as it is
  %%> - `incus pause`, then a reclaim of the container's memory
  %%> - the processes and their state stay; their memory goes to swap, about 13 MiB stays in RAM for a clone, 78 MiB for a nested-fleet default
  %%> - a frozen or stopped container stays as it is
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the reclaim follows the pause, so no running process pulls a page back
  %%> - with only the outer cgroup open, 1,531 MiB of a nested default stayed in RAM: a cgroup with 0 keeps its pages
  %%> - swap opens only while the Instance sleeps: a running container never swaps
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - a failed swap write or pause writes 0 back to each cgroup it opened and removes the marker, `instance.container_freeze_failed`; the container serves as before
  opt more than 32 MiB of anonymous memory stays in RAM
    alt a nested-fleet Instance
      SW->>N: Resume, never stop<br/>Ansible · resume, close swap
      %%> Why:
      %%> - a stop loses the Club key in /run and the tunnel `wg-nested`; only a deployment or a start gives them back
      %%> Result:
      %%> - the container runs again, swap closes on each cgroup the freeze opened, the marker goes; the run fails `instance.container_freeze_failed`
      %%> - the Instance serves as before; free swap and run the command again
    else any other Instance
      SW->>N: Stop the container<br/>Ansible · resume, incus stop
      %%> Why:
      %%> - swap is full; a frozen container would hold RAM, which a sleep exists to free
      %%> Result:
      %%> - `sleep: stopped`; the next wake boots it behind the same marker
      %%> On failure:
      %%> - the resumed container runs: swap closes again, the marker goes, `instance.container_freeze_failed`
    end
  end
  alt the run passes
    %% path: main
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal result
    Note over GW: Record asleep
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the runtime's `power_state` is `stopped` for a frozen and a stopped container; the result says `sleep: frozen` or `sleep: stopped`
    %%> - `t3_turn` is `idle` when the idle sleep read T3 first, `no_server` for an Instance without a T3 server, and `not_checked` for the command
    %%> - the Conn layer shows its T3 server asleep; the hourly renewal skips it
  else a step fails
    %% path: failed
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code
    %%> Result:
    %%> - `power_state` stays as it was. A marker left on a running container is harmless: the next request wakes the Instance, which removes it
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - the same command again
  end
  loop until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK
    GW-->>CLI: Status and result
  end
